    "question": {
      ":question/id": "83ea58c0-ecf6-59c2-a045-c00f94a73059",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2 \\sin {t},\\,y=\\frac{6 t^{2}}{\\pi},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 \\cos {t},\\,y'(t)=\\frac{12 t}{\\pi}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{12 t}{\\pi}\\right)}{\\left(2 \\cos {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6 t \\sec {t}}{\\pi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6 \\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\sec {\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right)}}{\\pi} \\\\  & =\\frac{6 \\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\left(- \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{\\pi} \\\\  & =- \\frac{15 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
